so can you just talk about like what  life looks like for you now with  quadruplets he has been to the circus  hello everybody we're the gardener's and  we have quadruplet three-year-old little  girls so when Ashley and I got married  we both knew that we want to have a big  family I'm the oldest of ten Ashley's  the youngest of five and when we decided  to start trying to have children it was  just part of a dream that we had a  vision we were patient in the beginning  we didn't really worry too much about it  we were young and we were still enjoying  life going into that first year of  trying we started to have some doubts  and we were beginning to be frustrated  so we decided to go to several different  doctors and we were just trying to  figure out what's going on I kind of  self diagnosed myself with in Demetrios  as' I had done some research and I  pretty much knew that I had all of these  symptoms no one would believe me and so  several years passed and we went to  several different doctors and they just  said well we don't know why just keep  trying and it was super frustrating it  was about 6 years in that we finally  found a doctor that agreed with me that  I had endometriosis and sent me to a  surgeon who did a laparoscopy surgery  that's the only way you can see if you  have endometriosis as if they go in  physically and look at it when she went  in for the surgery she predicted that I  probably had about a stage 2  endometriosis and when she got out of  the surgery she realized I had a stage 3  borderline for endometriosis and that  means that basically I just have scar  tissue all over my reproductive organs  which was keeping us from getting  pregnant she cleaned out as much as she  could and and gave us the best chances  of getting pregnant on her own at that  point we tried for another year and we  still didn't get pregnant we started to  look into adoption and I just had this  really strong gut feeling that I just  needed to try IVF one time both of them  are very expensive time consuming a lot  goes into that process and so you know  making that decision for us was one of  the hardest decisions that we had to  make at that time you know a lot of  infertility is not covered by insurance  and so we knew we were kind of on her  own so we decided to go in and tell our  doctor we were ready to do IVF we had  saved up the money for it that is not an  easy process to do at all it is very  draining I mean you're giving yourself  shots every single day it's extremely  painful to go through we got through the  process  all of our eggs ended up not making it  except for the two so they put the two  back and we waited he gave us a 40%  chance of having one baby  those were the the odds that they gave  us so I mean that was a very very  emotional and tough day for both of us  if this didn't work we were just gonna  go on to adoption which was going to be  great during that time we had a lot of  fears and doubts that were starting to  creep in that we were just gonna have  one and I wanted to have twins at least  I was praying for twins we go in for the  ultrasound and she starts looking and  she immediately sees two sacks and she  goes oh you guys have twins we were  amazed we were so pumped and she goes  wait hold on a second she keeps getting  closer and closer to the screen and I  looked at Tyson and honestly my first  thought was oh my gosh like one or both  of them don't have heartbeats  and it felt like an eternity before she  finally was like there's four in there  and I was like oh my gosh is this real  life I mean I'd never known anybody who  had had quadruplets before I was so  excited because this was it for us  and like we're gonna get our whole  family but it's my body able to do this  are the babies gonna be okay  obviously coming out of that ultrasound  like we didn't even know which way to  turn this was something I mean so  unexpected luckily for us both of our  families live close our brothers or  sisters are here our parents and we  relied on them heavily the pregnancy  just kind of was a whirlwind from there  you don't really have time to sit back  and enjoy the moment of jeez we're  having quadruplets let's think about  this for a minute I mean you just go go  go go go the doctors want to see you  every week it is a high-risk pregnancy  and they want to check and look at those  babies as much as they can  everything had looked great and all the  babies were growing perfectly their  heartbeats were perfect everything was  great I went in at 20 weeks for an  appointment and everything went  completely the opposite direction they  had been watching for something called  TTTS  which is twin to twin transfusion  syndrome when TTTS happens it means that  their blood vessels have fused together  and they're sharing nutrition which is  fine as long as they keep sharing it  back and forth and as me had given all  of the nutrients to ND and indi wasn't  passing any of it back and so this means  that indy had  ton of fluid around her to the point  where her heart was beating overtime as  she was about to go into heart failure  and as me we couldn't even see her  individual sac that she was in it was  suction cup to her body and they  couldn't find a bladder on her so she  was technically starving to death we  found out that day that there are  incredible doctors who do these  life-saving fetal surgeries no one in  Utah did it so we had to fly to LA to  get a surgery from an incredible doctor  there he drained a liter of fluid off of  Indy's sac which was a ton for a little  tiny baby they use a laser and they  separate the blood vessels so this  surgery was either going to work and it  was going to save all of these babies  lives or it was going to cost so much  trauma that I was going to just deliver  and we would have lost them all and  there was just a 50/50 chance and we  just had to go with it and go with our  faith in prayer that everything was  going to be okay  they did the surgery we waited 24 hours  we went and did another ultrasound and  all four of these little miracle babies  had survived that very traumatic surgery  and we flew back home and I was on bed  rest from then until I delivered so an  average day for us is the girls wake up  about eight o'clock and we wake up we  bring them downstairs we cook them all  breakfast and you try and get  two-year-olds to eat which they don't me  as a paranoid mom I'm always running  around with like snacks and fruit and  just eat something we always try to do  an activity in the morning whether it's  a park or yeah we go out taking them on  a little shopping trip to where they can  get out and run their energy out so yeah  that's what we do and then we come home  we eat lunch and the girls go down for a  nap still we are lucky with that not all  two-year-olds almost three-year-olds nap  that's when we entice and where then  they wake up and we just play and do  dinner and then play more do bath time  and then it's off to bed and then the  end dad work again you know when the  babies were born it was just such a  miracle honestly I think God just looked  out for us he blessed us every step of  the way he really did I mean every  doctor we've come in contact with has  said your girls shouldn't be they  shouldn't be healthy or you know some of  them made shouldn't have even been here  they truly are miracles and they have a  purpose for being here because they have  defied every odd that was thrown at them  I mean when they were born Indy was  smallest and she got down to one pound 6  ounces she just fit in the palm of your  hand  they were so strong and we had  incredible doctors and staff for those  of you guys who are still struggling  with infertility there are definitely  times when you don't think it's going to  end and there's definitely times when  you lose your faith just keep putting  one foot in front of the other and take  it one step at a time you just have to  remember that everything is going to be  ok and your story is going to be  beautiful just for you and it's going to  turn out the exact way that it is  supposed to for you all right so these  are our quadruplet daughters and as you  can see sometimes you have to bribe them  a little bit or you got to get on your  horse  show me how you rock yeah  [Applause]  [Music]

